a node's symbol is not required, its removal might cause a required symbol to become absent? That doesn't make sense because removal only removes its own symbol. The only way removal can affect required symbols is if the node itself is a required symbol, but we skip those. So the check is effectively: we only remove nodes whose symbol is not required. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically satisfied if we skip required nodes. But what about the possibility that removing a node might cause its child that is a required symbol to become disconnected? But the tree is only a set of nodes; we are not removing children, only the node itself. The child nodes are still present as separate nodes; they are not dependent on the parent for existence. The problem says \"Never remove a node with retained children\" which ensures we don't remove a parent that still has children, but that is about children, not about required symbols. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is only about the symbols of the nodes themselves, not about connectivity. So indeed, if we skip required symbol nodes, it's fine. However, we must also consider that some required symbol node might be a child of the node we are removing. That doesn't affect the presence of that child's symbol because we are not removing the child. So the condition holds. So we can simplify: only skip if node's symbol is in required_symbols or root or not removable or has retained children. No extra check needed.\n\nBut the instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Possibly they want us to simulate removal and check that the set of symbols of retained nodes still includes all required symbols. Even if the node's symbol is not required, removal could remove the only occurrence of some required symbol if that required symbol node is a descendant? No, because we are not removing descendants, only the node itself. So that