 of a node: children_by_parent[node['id']] gives list of child ids. Then check if any child id is in retained.\n\nBut careful: When we remove a node, its children become orphans? That's fine, they remain as separate nodes. They are still in retained. So children of removed nodes are still retained, but their parent is removed. That's allowed. So when checking \"node with retained children\", we only care about direct children that are still retained. So after removal of some child, parent's children set may shrink. Since we process deeper first, child nodes are processed before parent. So when we get to parent, some children may have been removed. So we need to dynamically check children_by_parent[parent_id] and see which are still in retained. Since children_by_parent is static, we just check membership in retained.\n\nNow, about the root: root has parent null, but we skip root.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    req_syms = set(case['required_symbols'])  # set for fast check\n    node_by_id = {}\n    children = defaultdict(list)\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\n        parent = n['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children[parent].append(nid)\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    removal_order = []\n\n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid == 'root':\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in req_syms:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # tentative preservation of required symbols: after removal, still present?\n        # Since we already skip required symbol nodes, this is always true, but check anyway\n        # Compute symbols of retained after removal\n        # But we can just check: if n['symbol'] in req_syms, but we already skipped, so safe.\n        # However, we might remove a node that has a required symbol child? That child is still present, so fine.\n        # We'll still do a check for completeness:\n        # temp_retained = retained - {nid}\n        # temp_symbols = set(node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] for rid in temp_retained)\n        # if not req_syms.issubset(temp_symbols):\n        #     continue\n        # Actually since we skip required symbol nodes, no need. But we can include.\n        # For speed, we'll keep it simple.\n        # Remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    # sort retained IDs\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nBut we need to be careful: The root is required? required_symbols includes \"root\". So root's symbol is \"root\" which is in required_symbols, so we skip it. Good.\n\nOne more nuance: The node with id \"root\" may have children. Since we never remove root, its children are safe.\n\nNow, about the \"removable\" flag: In the fixture, some nodes have removable:false. Should we attempt to remove those? The instructions say \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It doesn't explicitly say \"only if removable\", but the field is present. In Perses, not all nodes are removable. The problem says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It doesn't mention removable. But the field is given, and it's a \"hard contract\". Likely we should respect the removable flag. The fixture shows n2 and n4 have removable true, n1 and n3 false. The required symbols are root and s1. So root is required, s1 is required. n1 has symbol s1, so it's required, so skip. n2 has symbol s2, removable true, so we can consider it. n3 has symbol s0, removable false, so we skip because not removable. n4 has symbol s1 (required) so skip. n5 has symbol s2, removable false, skip. n6 has symbol s0, removable true, so consider. So indeed we only consider nodes that are both removable and not required and not root and no retained children.\n\nNow, depth: root depth 0, n1 depth1, n2 depth1, n3 depth1, n4 depth2, n5 depth2, n6 depth2. Sorted by depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4,n5,n6; then depth1: n1,n2,n3; then root depth0. Among depth2, tie-breaking by id: n4,n5,n6. So order: n4, n5, n6, then n1, n2, n3. Let's simulate first case. n4: symbol s1, required, skip. n5: symbol s2, removable false, skip (if we check removable). n6: symbol s0, removable true, no retained children? n6 has parent n1, but its children? In fixture, n6 has no children (since no node has parent n6). So retained children? children[ n6 ] is empty list, so no children. So can remove? Check required symbols: after removal, s0 is not required, so fine. Remove n6. Then retained set reduces. Then n1: symbol s1, required, skip. n2: symbol s2, removable true, children? children[n2] = ['n4']. n4 is still retained because we haven't removed n4 (it's required, so not removed). So n2 has a retained child n4. So skip. n3: symbol s0, removable false, skip. Then root skip. So removal order: ['n6']. Retained IDs: all except n6: root, n1,n2,n3,n4,n5. Sorted: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root. That seems plausible.\n\nNow, we need to output the Python source as a single string inside JSON. We'll escape it properly. We'll write the code as a string and then encode.\n\nFinal answer: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    from collections import defaultdict\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    req_syms = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(nid)\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removal_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == 'root':\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in req_syms:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"}\n\nBut we need to ensure the JSON string is properly escaped. We'll use a JSON encoder or manually escape. Since we are writing the response, we can just output the JSON object with the string containing newlines and quotes escaped. We'll double-check.\n\nWe'll produce the final response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1849,"completion_tokens":4268,"total_tokens":6117,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3992},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":57},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}